WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.797
ROGER MARTINEZ: Whether
you're a gigantic enterprise

00:00:02.797 --> 00:00:05.790
or a small team at a startup
or even a lone developer,

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.740
if you're using
Google Cloud, there's

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.870
one thing you all
have in common.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:14.070
Your project will use and
depend on service accounts.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.845
Everyone uses them,
but what are they?

00:00:16.845 --> 00:00:18.630
What do they do?

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.890
Where do they come from?

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.995
And why should you care?

00:00:21.995 --> 00:00:23.370
These are all
important questions

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.800
that we have got to
know the answers to.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.540
I'm here to help you
figure out what's

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.550
what with service accounts.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.812
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.653
So service accounts--
what exactly are they?

00:00:37.653 --> 00:00:39.570
To answer that, let's
take a look at something

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.430
you might already
be familiar with--

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.636
a user account.

00:00:42.636 --> 00:00:45.700
[AUDIO OUT] account represents
Yajaira, the lead engineer

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.170
on your team.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:50.320
She accesses the account with
the email address and password

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.360
associated with it,
and access to it

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.195
is shared with no one else.

00:00:54.195 --> 00:00:56.450
[AUDIO OUT] IAM rules
associated with it

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.820
that grant it permission
to do some fun stuff,

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.180
like manage Kubernetes clusters,
create compute instances,

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.865
and configure monitoring
on the project.

00:01:04.865 --> 00:01:06.860
As long as Yajaira
works on the team,

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.800
she can do whatever
the IAM policy allows.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.560
And if she leaves a
team, well, her account

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.380
would be disabled so that
it couldn't be used anymore.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.900
That's a user account.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.740
A service account,
on the other hand,

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.970
is an identity that a compute
instance or an application

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:26.123
can use to run API
requests on your behalf.

00:01:26.123 --> 00:01:29.120
[AUDIO OUT] are a few major
differences between a user

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.390
account and a service account.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.670
Like Yahaira's user
account, a service account

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.110
has an assigned email.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.420
But instead of a
password, it can only

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.720
be authenticated using a
private-public RSA key pair,

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.611
and it can't be logged
in via a browser.

00:01:44.611 --> 00:01:47.600
[AUDIO OUT] accounts typically
represent virtual machines

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.870
and applications.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.050
And while user accounts
should never be shared,

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.720
permission can be granted to
users to impersonate service

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.630
accounts, allowing multiple
users to access everything

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.555
that a service
account can access.

00:01:58.555 --> 00:02:00.870
[AUDIO OUT] accounts
are granted IAM roles

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.780
to perform certain actions
and access certain resources

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.790
in your project, just
like a user account,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.880
although they're usually
executing automated actions

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.910
and might have multiple
processes dependent on them.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.100
That means you'll need
to be extra careful when

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.042
you edit their access or if you
want to disable or delete them.

00:02:19.042 --> 00:02:21.180
[AUDIO OUT] two types
of service accounts.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.340
Well, first, there are
Google-managed service

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.540
accounts.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.400
You might see these sometimes
referred to as service agents.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.740
These are service accounts
that google automatically

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.280
creates and manages for
some Google Cloud Services.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.650
For example, right off
the bat, your project

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:40.310
is likely to have a
Google APIs service agent.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:43.220
This is a service account that
runs internal Google processes

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.120
on your behalf.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.280
There could be more
Google-managed service accounts

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.740
that exist in your
project, but you'll only

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:53.010
see them in your IAM
policy or audit logs

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.466
when you enable an API or
a service that needs it.

00:02:56.466 --> 00:02:58.970
[AUDIO OUT] service
accounts, you technically

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.960
can change and
revoke their roles.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.407
But that's generally
not a great idea

00:03:03.407 --> 00:03:05.240
because it could cause
some services to stop

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.545
working correctly.

00:03:06.545 --> 00:03:08.300
[AUDIO OUT] type
of service account

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.280
that we have is a
user-managed service account.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.160
This is a service account
that you explicitly

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.560
create using identity
and access management.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.116
You choose a name and the roles
associated with it yourself.

00:03:20.116 --> 00:03:22.150
[AUDIO OUT] into
any that you create,

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.700
user-managed service accounts
include default service

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:25.570
accounts.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.265
These are user-managed
service accounts

00:03:27.265 --> 00:03:30.160
that are created when you use
some Google Cloud Services that

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.380
are meant to help
you to get started.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.030
A default service
account is created

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:37.150
for App Engine and any
service that uses App Engine

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.580
and for Compute Engine
and any service that

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.050
uses Compute Engine.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.720
[AUDIO OUT] for those default
service accounts is typically

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.800
PROJECT_NUMBER-c
ompute@developer

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:51.265
.gserviceaccount.com for
Compute Engine and PROJECT

00:03:51.265 --> 00:03:55.630
ID@appspot.gserviceaccount.com
for App Engine.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:58.180
Default service accounts are
generally granted the editor

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.070
role on your project.

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.460
For production workloads,
this might be a bit much.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.860
So it's recommended that
you create your own service

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.590
accounts for these services
and apply more restricted roles

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:09.840
to them.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.430
How can we see these
service accounts

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.727
in our Google Cloud project?

00:04:13.727 --> 00:04:15.810
If you want to see all of
the user-managed service

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.010
accounts that currently
exist on your project,

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.490
you can use the Google
Cloud console on their IAM

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.620
or through a CLI using
the gcloud command.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.920
The command $ gcloud iam
service-accounts list will

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.170
nicely list your user
managed service accounts,

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:32.225
display names, emails, and
whether they're disabled.

00:04:32.225 --> 00:04:34.150
[AUDIO OUT] see all of
your service accounts,

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.230
including Google-managed ones,
the command $ gcloud projects

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:41.370
get-iam-policy followed by your
project ID is your best bet.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.920
This will spit out your
project's IAM policy,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.360
the roles currently assigned to
the accounts on your project.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.860
This is going to include user
accounts, user-managed service

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.230
accounts, and Google-managed
service accounts,

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.380
as long as they have
roles assigned to them.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.130
[AUDIO OUT] have only begun to
scratch the surface of service

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.335
accounts here.

00:04:58.335 --> 00:04:59.710
There are still
some other things

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.800
worth considering when we
talk about service accounts.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.720
In this series, we're
going to learn more

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.610
about creating them,
getting rid of them,

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:09.400
effectively managing them,
properly securing them,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.130
and using them for development.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:14.490
